      "summary": "Automate checks and use AI and cloud development tools to move from idea to reviewed change.",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "slug": "gh-actions-workflow-basics",
          "title": "GitHub Actions workflow basics",
          "objectiveCode": "4.1",
          "estimatedMinutes": 12,
          "summary": "Learn how workflows, events, jobs, steps, and runners fit together in GitHub Actions automation.",
          "learningObjectives": [
            "Describe workflows and events",
            "Identify jobs, steps, and runners",
            "Read a simple workflow YAML example",
            "Connect the Actions lab to troubleshooting"
          ],
          "explanation": "When a team says, 'Can we stop forgetting to run tests?', **GitHub Actions** is the helpful teammate that never gets bored. A workflow is an automated process stored as YAML in `.github/workflows`. It starts when an **event** happens, such as `push`, `pull_request`, a schedule, or a manual dispatch. A workflow contains **jobs**. Each job runs on a **runner**, the machine that executes the job, and contains **steps** that run commands or reusable actions.\n\nRead workflow YAML like a map: `on` means when, `jobs` means what work, `runs-on` means where, and `steps` means how. A real Node.js workflow might run on pull requests, use `actions/checkout`, set up Node.js, run `npm ci`, and then run `npm test`. GitHub-hosted runners provide ready machines; self-hosted runners are managed by your organization.\n\nThe `Create and troubleshoot a GitHub Actions workflow` lab lets you configure a Node.js workflow, inspect a failed run, fix the test failure, and verify success.",
          "terminology": [
            {
              "term": "Workflow",
              "definition": "YAML-defined automated process in a repository."
            },
            {
              "term": "Event",
              "definition": "Trigger that starts a workflow."
            },
            {
              "term": "Job",
              "definition": "Group of steps that runs on a runner."
            },
            {
              "term": "Step",
              "definition": "Command or reusable action inside a job."
            },
            {
              "term": "Runner",
              "definition": "Machine that executes a job."
            }
          ],
          "businessScenario": "Contoso wants every pull request to install dependencies and run tests before review.",
          "technicalExample": "```yaml\nname: Node CI\non: [pull_request]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm test\n```",
          "misconception": {
            "myth": "Actions is only for deployments.",
            "reality": "Actions can test, build, label, scan, publish, deploy, and automate many repository tasks."
          },
          "examTakeaway": "Map scenario words: pull request = event, hosted machine = runner, commands = steps, grouped work = job.",
          "recap": "Learn how workflows, events, jobs, steps, and runners fit together in GitHub Actions automation.",
          "simplerExplanation": "Picture GitHub Actions as an automatic quality gate at a workshop door. When someone brings in a pull request, the gate checks the checklist: get the code, install parts, run tests, and show a log before a reviewer spends time on it.",
          "anotherExample": "A docs site can use one workflow for pull requests that checks Markdown links, and a second workflow on release tags that publishes the site. Same building blocks, different event and job purpose.",
          "extraBlocks": [
            {
              "type": "COMPARISON",
              "data": {
                "title": "Workflow building blocks",
                "columns": [
                  {
