          "slug": "ai-cloud-development-environments",
          "title": "Copilot, Codespaces, and github.dev",
          "objectiveCode": "4.1",
          "estimatedMinutes": 12,
          "summary": "Choose between GitHub Copilot, GitHub Codespaces, and github.dev for common modern development needs.",
          "learningObjectives": [
            "Explain Copilot assistance",
            "Describe Codespaces and dev containers",
            "Differentiate github.dev from Codespaces",
            "Select the right tool for a scenario"
          ],
          "explanation": "Modern development is about removing friction between an idea and a reviewed change. **GitHub Copilot** can suggest code, explain unfamiliar files, draft tests, and support AI-assisted development workflows. **GitHub Codespaces** gives developers a cloud-hosted environment connected to a repository. A **dev container** can define tools, runtimes, extensions, and settings so everyone starts consistently.\n\n**github.dev** is different: it opens a repository in VS Code for the Web. It is great for reading code, editing Markdown, and making small browser-only changes. When a scenario needs a terminal, dependencies, or a repeatable environment, choose Codespaces. When it needs a quick browser edit, choose github.dev.\n\nFor GH-900, focus on capability clues: AI assistance points to Copilot, cloud development environments point to Codespaces, and lightweight browser editing points to github.dev.",
          "terminology": [
            {
              "term": "GitHub Copilot",
              "definition": "AI assistant for code suggestions and development help."
            },
            {
              "term": "Codespace",
              "definition": "Cloud-hosted development environment."
            },
            {
              "term": "Dev container",
              "definition": "Configuration for tools and runtime in a development container."
            },
